WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.857
Imagine a document service where a student
successfully logs in and requests another

00:00:04.857 --> 00:00:10.119
student's private submission. Authentication can
correctly identify the requester while the

00:00:10.119 --> 00:00:15.381
requested read must still be denied. Put a guard
before the document operation: it receives

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:19.197
the authenticated identity, the action, and the
specific document.

00:00:19.197 --> 00:00:24.582
The policy might permit the owner and assigned
marker to read, but nobody else. Draw those

00:00:24.582 --> 00:00:30.027
inputs separately so identity is not mistaken
for authority. Confidentiality is violated if

00:00:30.027 --> 00:00:35.173
the read succeeds incorrectly; integrity is
violated if an unauthorized edit succeeds.

00:00:35.197 --> 00:00:40.484
Availability asks whether legitimate readers can
still obtain access. A useful threat model

00:00:40.484 --> 00:00:45.654
specifies which requests an attacker can send
and which boundaries the system promises to

00:00:45.654 --> 00:00:46.119
enforce.

00:00:46.197 --> 00:00:51.007
A permission check can be undermined if later
code lets untrusted input rewrite the

00:00:51.007 --> 00:00:56.281
operation being checked. Suppose a profile query
is intended to select only public records.

00:00:56.281 --> 00:01:01.206
If the application builds SQL by joining
strings, hostile input may alter the query's

00:01:01.206 --> 00:01:03.467
condition instead of remaining a value.

00:01:03.697 --> 00:01:08.800
The guard may have approved a public-profile
lookup while the database executes a broader

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.845
query. Parameter binding keeps the query
structure fixed and supplies input as data. The

00:01:13.845 --> 00:01:18.775
architectural lesson extends beyond this
example: validate the operation that actually

00:01:18.775 --> 00:01:23.247
reaches the protected resource, and avoid
alternate paths that skip mediation.

00:01:23.247 --> 00:01:28.722
Test a denied request, a malformed request, and
an allowed request so the evidence covers

00:01:28.722 --> 00:01:31.490
the boundary rather than only successful use.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:36.532
A backup worker needs to read production data
and create a protected copy. Giving it the

00:01:36.532 --> 00:01:41.573
administrator account also lets it delete
production records, disable auditing, or erase

00:01:41.573 --> 00:01:46.500
every backup. Those powers are unrelated to its
purpose. Draw separate credentials for

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:51.656
reading production and writing a backup
destination, then remove deletion rights where
the

00:01:51.656 --> 00:01:53.260
workflow does not need them.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:58.959
If an attacker compromises the worker, the
remaining damage is bounded by those
permissions.

00:01:58.959 --> 00:02:03.893
Least privilege is therefore a design decision
about component responsibilities and

00:02:03.893 --> 00:02:09.244
interfaces, not merely a checkbox on an account.
Follow the recovery path too: a backup is

00:02:09.244 --> 00:02:14.356
useful only if the recovery process can access a
trustworthy copy after the production

00:02:14.356 --> 00:02:15.664
system is compromised.

00:02:15.664 --> 00:02:20.635
Consider an authorization service that is
temporarily unavailable. If an application

00:02:20.635 --> 00:02:25.962
interprets a timeout as permission, an outage
becomes an access bypass. A fail-safe design

00:02:25.962 --> 00:02:30.933
rejects the protected operation or uses an
explicitly bounded, previously authorized

00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:33.656
decision whose freshness rules are understood.

00:02:33.664 --> 00:02:39.140
Complete mediation requires examining every
route, including batch jobs and internal APIs,

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:44.191
rather than protecting only the public page.
Defense in depth then adds independent

00:02:44.191 --> 00:02:49.546
boundaries: a narrow service identity, network
restrictions, and storage permissions can

00:02:49.546 --> 00:02:52.224
still constrain damage when one check fails.

00:02:52.224 --> 00:02:57.489
These controls must remain understandable to
operators. A complicated emergency procedure

00:02:57.489 --> 00:03:02.695
that routinely requires disabling all checks
creates an operational weakness even if the

00:03:02.695 --> 00:03:05.121
normal architecture diagram looks secure.

00:03:05.224 --> 00:03:10.572
Suppose a database leak exposes account records.
If the records contain plain passwords, the

00:03:10.572 --> 00:03:15.688
attacker can immediately try them on other
services where people reused those passwords.

00:03:15.688 --> 00:03:20.804
Password verification should instead compare
against a dedicated password-hashing result

00:03:20.804 --> 00:03:22.606
with a unique salt per account.

00:03:22.724 --> 00:03:28.055
During login, the server repeats the configured
calculation using the submitted password and

00:03:28.055 --> 00:03:33.039
stored salt. The salt is not a secret
replacement password: its role is to prevent one

00:03:33.039 --> 00:03:37.965
reusable precomputed result from serving many
accounts. An expensive password-hashing

00:03:37.965 --> 00:03:41.269
function increases the work required for offline
guesses.

00:03:41.269 --> 00:03:46.170
Rate limiting helps with guesses sent through
the login endpoint, but it cannot control an

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:50.146
attacker computing guesses independently from a
stolen verifier database.

00:03:50.269 --> 00:03:55.444
Trace a password from the browser to the login
service over HTTPS. Transport protection

00:03:55.444 --> 00:04:00.619
prevents an observer along that connection from
simply reading or changing the request,

00:04:00.619 --> 00:04:05.676
provided the peer and connection are correctly
verified. Once the request reaches the

00:04:05.676 --> 00:04:10.078
application, however, the plaintext password may
be available to its code.

00:04:10.269 --> 00:04:15.353
Logging the entire request can copy that secret
into another system with different access

00:04:15.353 --> 00:04:20.266
controls and retention. Requiring the password
on every API operation multiplies these

00:04:20.266 --> 00:04:25.008
opportunities and forces the client to keep a
powerful long-lived secret available.

00:04:25.008 --> 00:04:29.807
Authenticate through the login workflow, then
use an appropriately protected session

00:04:29.807 --> 00:04:31.635
credential for later operations.

00:04:31.769 --> 00:04:37.338
Transport security, secret handling, and account
verification solve different parts of the

00:04:37.338 --> 00:04:38.143
same journey.

00:04:38.269 --> 00:04:43.510
After a successful login, the backend generates
an unpredictable session identifier and

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:48.570
stores its association with the user and expiry.
The client sends that identifier on

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:53.751
subsequent requests. In a feed request, the API
first translates the token into a user

00:04:53.751 --> 00:04:59.173
identity, then reads the feed for that identity;
it should not trust a separately supplied

00:04:59.173 --> 00:05:01.041
username as proof of ownership.

00:05:01.269 --> 00:05:06.412
A cookie can carry the identifier automatically
for a browser, while another client may use

00:05:06.412 --> 00:05:11.330
an authorization header. These are transport
choices around the same underlying session

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:16.530
idea. Revoking the session invalidates that
credential without changing the user's memorized

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:17.039
password.

00:05:17.269 --> 00:05:22.104
The token is still a secret: possession can be
enough to impersonate the session while it

00:05:22.104 --> 00:05:22.865
remains valid.

00:05:22.865 --> 00:05:27.802
Now place a cache in front of session
verification so every API request does not make

00:05:27.802 --> 00:05:32.332
another network round trip. Performance
improves, but a session revoked at the

00:05:32.332 --> 00:05:37.094
authentication service may still be accepted by
a cache holding an older decision.

00:05:37.094 --> 00:05:42.059
Choose a bounded lifetime or invalidation
mechanism that matches the harm of stale

00:05:42.059 --> 00:05:47.085
authorization. A temporary browsing session and
an administrator action may require

00:05:47.085 --> 00:05:52.292
different policies. Keep tokens out of ordinary
URLs because request paths often reach

00:05:52.292 --> 00:05:54.714
histories, logs, and monitoring systems.

00:05:54.714 --> 00:06:00.289
A second factor adds another check, but recovery
must protect the same account: a weak reset

00:06:00.289 --> 00:06:05.439
process can bypass a strong login. Evaluate both
normal access and credential loss as

00:06:05.439 --> 00:06:09.560
complete workflows, including revocation and
renewed authentication.
